  "full_response": "The **CAP theorem**, also known as **Brewer's theorem**, is a fundamental principle in distributed systems that states it is impossible for a distributed data store to simultaneously provide all three of the following guarantees:\n\n1. **Consistency (C)** – Every read receives the most recent write or an error. All nodes see the same data at the same time.\n2. **Availability (A)** – Every request receives a (non-error) response, even if some nodes fail. The system remains operational.\n3. **Partition Tolerance (P)** – The system continues to operate despite network partitions (communication failures between nodes).\n\n### **Implications of the CAP Theorem**\nSince network partitions (P) are inevitable in distributed systems, the theorem implies that designers must choose between **consistency (C)** and **availability (A)** when a partition occurs. This leads to three possible system types:\n\n1. **CP (Consistency + Partition Tolerance)**\n   - Sacrifices availability to ensure consistency.\n   - If a partition occurs, the system may block or return errors until consistency is restored.\n   - Example: **MongoDB (in strong consistency mode), HBase, ZooKeeper, etcd.**\n\n2. **AP (Availability + Partition Tolerance)**\n   - Sacrifices consistency to remain available.\n   - If a partition occurs, the system continues serving requests, but some nodes may return stale data.\n   - Example: **Cassandra, DynamoDB (eventual consistency), CouchDB, Riak.**\n\n3. **CA (Consistency + Availability)**\n   - Sacrifices partition tolerance (theoretical, not practical in distributed systems).\n   - Works only in single-node systems or fully reliable networks (rare in real-world distributed databases).\n   - Example: **Traditional relational databases (PostgreSQL, MySQL) in non-distributed setups.**\n\n### **Key Takeaways for Distributed Database Design**\n1. **Partition Tolerance is Non-Negotiable**\n   - Since network failures are inevitable, **P must always be chosen**, leaving a trade-off between **C and A**.\n\n2. **Eventual Consistency is Common in AP Systems**\n   - Many modern distributed databases (e.g., Cassandra, DynamoDB) prioritize availability and use **eventual consistency**, where updates propagate asynchronously.\n\n3. **Strong Consistency Requires Sacrificing Availability**\n   - Systems like **etcd** or **ZooKeeper** enforce strong consistency but may become",
